      <header class="cod-title-block">
        <p class="cod-chapter-label">Chapter Nine · Spirit of Truth or Seducing Spirits?</p>
        <h1 class="cod-title">Ministries</h1>
        <p class="cod-subtitle">Whose Temple Are You Keeping?</p>
        <p class="cod-tagline">Everyone in the church has a ministry now. Scripture gave every believer one. They are not the same ministry.</p>
      </header>

      <p class="cod-pullquote">Spirit of Truth or Seducing Spirits? Let Scripture and history decide.</p>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">INTRODUCTION</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Question No One Is Asking</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">In almost any church today, every job has the same name: the administrative ministry, the worship ministry, the greeting ministry, the children's, youth, media, and hospitality ministries, even a parking ministry. Outside the building there are ministries too — a man's name followed by the word <em>Ministries</em>, with a website and a page asking for monthly partners. A young man "called to the ministry" is going to seminary to become a pastor.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">In Scripture, <em>ministry</em> never meant a church job, a career, or an organization. It meant the service of a priest — and in the new covenant, every believer is the priest, and the temple he serves is his own body.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Scripture names the power behind a practice that turns believers from the truth: "some shall depart from the faith, giving heed to seducing spirits" (1 Timothy 4:1). Against it stands the Spirit of truth, who "will guide you into all truth" (John 16:13). In the altar call, the enemy erased the blood. <strong>In the ministry system, he erased the walk.</strong> Obedience to Yahuah was replaced, step by step, with activity done for Him.</p>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">I</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">What Ministry Means</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">In the Old Testament, the priests and Levites "minister" — the Hebrew <em>sharath</em>, to serve in the holy place (Exodus 28:43). Their first duty is named again and again with one word: <em>shamar</em>, to guard and keep. "Ye shall keep the charge of the sanctuary" (Numbers 18:5). The words go back to the garden, where Yahuah put Adam "to dress it and to keep it" (Genesis 2:15) — <em>avad</em> and <em>shamar</em>, the same pair later used for the Levites. The first man was given a priest's work.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">The New Testament carries the same idea in Greek: <em>diakonia</em>, service, the word Yahushua used of Himself — "the Son of man came not to be ministered unto, but to minister" (Mark 10:45) — and <em>latreia</em>, the service of the sanctuary. Paul put that service in the believer's own body: "present your bodies a living sacrifice, holy, acceptable unto God, which is your reasonable service [<em>latreia</em>]" (Romans 12:1). "Know ye not that your body is the temple of the Holy Ghost which is in you?" (1 Corinthians 6:19). "If any man defile the temple of God, him shall God destroy; for the temple of God is holy, which temple ye are" (1 Corinthians 3:17). The believer's first ministry is Adam's and the Levite's: keep the charge of the temple — guard what goes into it, keep it clean, keep the lamp burning, and offer his life as the incense.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Priests also served people. They were set apart "to stand before the congregation to minister unto them" (Numbers 16:9), and the Law says which people: Yahuah "doth execute the judgment of the fatherless and widow, and loveth the stranger" (Deuteronomy 10:18), and the tithe fed "the stranger, and the fatherless, and the widow" (Deuteronomy 14:29). Acts 6 is that command carried out — hungry widows fed by men "full of the Holy Ghost and wisdom," chosen by the assembly. One of them, Stephen, went from that table to his witness and his death. Running a program inside a building is something else. Yahuah asked a people busy at His house, "who hath required this at your hand, to tread my courts? … seek judgment, relieve the oppressed, judge the fatherless, plead for the widow" (Isaiah 1:12, 17). This is the ministry the Spirit of truth gives: a kept temple first, and from it, service to the widow, the orphan, and the stranger.</p>

      <p class="cod-pullquote"><strong>Ministry in Scripture is the priest keeping the temple. In the new covenant, the priest is the believer, and the temple is his own body.</strong></p>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">II</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Servant Becomes a Rank (A.D. 96-400)</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">Around A.D. 96, a letter from the assembly at Rome, <em>1 Clement</em>, held up the order of the old temple — high priest, priests, Levites, and "the layman" (<em>laikos</em>) — as the model for the assembly. It is the earliest known use of <em>layman</em> in Christian writing. By the 200s the leaders were the <em>clergy</em>, from <em>klēros</em>, "inheritance," and everyone else the <em>laity</em> — though Scripture gives the inheritance to every believer (Colossians 1:12). In 313 Constantine made the clergy a privileged class of the empire.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Yahushua had set the opposite pattern: "the princes of the Gentiles exercise dominion over them … But it shall not be so among you: but whosoever will be great among you, let him be your minister [<em>diakonos</em>]" (Matthew 20:25-26). "One is your Master, even Messiah; and all ye are brethren" (Matthew 23:8). John met the opposite spirit in one man: "Diotrephes, who loveth to have the preeminence among them" (3 John 9). <strong>Wherever servants become a ruling class, a seducing spirit is at work.</strong> Here the walk began to slip. Once ministry was a rank, a man could stand close to Yahuah by holding the position, whether or not he kept the commandments.</p>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">III</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Calling (1500s-1800s)</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">In 1520 Martin Luther taught the priesthood of all believers. The Reformers removed what Luther protested — the sale of indulgences and the rule of the pope — and kept most of the structure they inherited, including Sunday and a separate clergy. The Augsburg Confession of 1530 said that no one should publicly teach in the church "unless he be regularly called." Those ranks went back to the early fathers; around A.D. 110 Ignatius of Antioch wrote, "Let no man do anything connected with the Church without the bishop." The Reformation broke with Rome's head and kept Rome's ladder.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Beside it grew the "call" to the ministry: an inner sense that a man was set apart to preach, which no one else could test. In America the calling became a career, with seminaries from 1808 on. Then came the religious societies — Bible, tract, and Sunday school societies run by boards and funded by donors, the first form of what is now called a ministry.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Scripture tells every believer, "believe not every spirit, but try the spirits whether they are of God" (1 John 4:1). A calling no one can test is the door Yahuah warned about: "I have not sent these prophets, yet they ran" (Jeremiah 23:21). "I sent them not, nor commanded them: therefore they shall not profit this people at all" (Jeremiah 23:32). <strong>A self-declared call, answering to donors instead of to an assembly, is a door a seducing spirit can walk through at will.</strong> A man was now judged by whether he felt called, not by whether he obeyed.</p>

      <details class="cod-deepdive">
        <summary>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-label">
            <span class="cod-deepdive-icon">✦</span>
            <span class="cod-deepdive-title">Go Deeper — The Benevolent Empire</span>
          </span>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-chevron">▾</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="cod-deepdive-body">
          <p class="cod-body">Between about 1810 and 1830, American Protestants built a network of national religious societies that historians call the "Benevolent Empire." The American Board of Commissioners for Foreign Missions was founded in 1810, the American Education Society in 1815 to pay the schooling of young men preparing for the ministry, the American Bible Society in 1816, the American Sunday School Union in 1824, the American Tract Society in 1825, and the American Home Missionary Society and the American Temperance Society in 1826.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">None of these was a church. Each was a corporation with a board of directors, a paid staff, a printing operation, and agents who traveled the country raising money. Many of the same wealthy men sat on several boards at once. In New York, the merchant brothers Arthur and Lewis Tappan gave large sums to a whole series of these societies. Every May the societies held their annual meetings in New York in the same week, with reports, speeches, and appeals for funds.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">The societies also set the pattern for raising money. They sold memberships, offered life memberships for a larger gift, printed the names of donors in their reports, and sent agents from church to church to take collections. The partner letters and monthly donor pages of later ministries grew out of these methods.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">Paul's collection for the saints at Jerusalem worked differently. The assemblies themselves gathered the gift on the first day of the week, chose the men to carry it, and sent it with letters, so that the givers could see it delivered (1 Corinthians 16:1-3; 2 Corinthians 8:19-21). There was no board, no agent, and no list of donors' names. The gift went from one assembly to another, carried by men the givers knew.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">IV</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Name on the Door (1870s-1930s)</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">By the late 1800s the minister was becoming a public name. Dwight L. Moody drew huge crowds in America and Britain, and the Chicago school he founded came to bear his name. Billy Sunday's city campaigns closed with a "freewill offering" for the evangelist himself. In 1923 Aimee Semple McPherson opened Angelus Temple in Los Angeles, and in 1924 launched her own radio station from it. <strong>The ministry and the man became the same thing.</strong> People no longer followed a teaching. They followed a name.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Paul refused to let anyone build on his name: "Is Messiah divided? was Paul crucified for you?" (1 Corinthians 1:13). "Who then is Paul, and who is Apollos, but ministers by whom ye believed" (1 Corinthians 3:5). The first great project of men who turned from Yahuah was a building and a name: "let us make us a name" (Genesis 11:4). Simon the sorcerer held Samaria by "giving out that himself was some great one" (Acts 8:9). <strong>A ministry named after a man is a seducing spirit's work: it points people to the man.</strong> People asked whose ministry it was, not whether anyone in it obeyed.</p>

      <details class="cod-deepdive">
        <summary>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-label">
            <span class="cod-deepdive-icon">✦</span>
            <span class="cod-deepdive-title">Go Deeper — When the Name Went on the Whole Church</span>
          </span>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-chevron">▾</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="cod-deepdive-body">
          <p class="cod-body">The New Testament knows one assembly in each city, named by its place: "the church of God which is at Corinth" (1 Corinthians 1:2), "the churches of Galatia" (Galatians 1:2), and the seven assemblies of Revelation, each named for its city (Revelation 2-3). None is named for a man, a practice, or a method.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>The names.</strong> In 1522 Martin Luther wrote, "I ask that men make no reference to my name; let them call themselves Christians, not Lutherans." His followers were called Lutherans anyway, and the name stuck. Other churches took their names from a practice (Baptist, after the baptism of believers; the first English Baptist congregation was formed in Amsterdam in 1609 under John Smyth), from a form of government (Presbyterian, from <em>presbyteros</em>, elder; Episcopal, from <em>episkopos</em>, overseer), or from a method. "Methodist" began as a mocking name for the Oxford students around John and Charles Wesley because of their strict rules of study and prayer. John Wesley lived and died a priest of the Church of England. In 1784, at the Christmas Conference in Baltimore, his American followers formed the Methodist Episcopal Church as a body of its own.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>The splits.</strong> Once the names were fixed, they divided again. American Presbyterians split into Old School and New School in 1837. In 1844 the Methodists divided North and South over slavery, and in 1845 the Southern Baptist Convention was formed out of the same quarrel. The Presbyterians split North and South again in 1861. Some of these divisions took a century to heal; the northern and southern Methodists did not reunite until 1939.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">New movements brought new names: the Seventh-day Adventists (1863), the Pentecostal churches that came out of Azusa Street (1906), the Assemblies of God (1914). The dividing has not stopped. Between 2019 and 2023, more than 7,600 congregations left the United Methodist Church.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">Paul answered it before it began: "For while one saith, I am of Paul; and another, I am of Apollos; are ye not carnal?" (1 Corinthians 3:4). Yahushua said, "if a kingdom be divided against itself, that kingdom cannot stand" (Mark 3:24). Jude described the dividers: "These be they who separate themselves, sensual, having not the Spirit" (Jude 19). The Spirit of truth makes one body (Ephesians 4:4). Division under men's names is the mark of another spirit.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">V</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">Partners, Corporations, and Titles for Sale (1945-Today)</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">After World War II, traveling healing evangelists held tent meetings across the country, and their ministries ran on radio, magazines, and mailing lists. Supporters were called "partners." The ministry was now an organization built around one man. Under the tax code of 1954, any religious ministry could incorporate as a nonprofit and give its donors tax-deductible receipts, and ministers received a tax-free housing allowance. In 1962 Kirby Hensley's Universal Life Church began ordaining anyone who asked, free, by mail; by 1974 it claimed more than a million ministers. The title had become a piece of paper.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Television built the largest ministries of all. Jim Bakker's PTL network sold more lifetime partnerships than its resort could ever hold. In 1987 Oral Roberts told his viewers that God would "call him home" unless they sent eight million dollars. Overseas ministries asked for trust the giver could never check: Gospel for Asia was expelled by the Evangelical Council for Financial Accountability in 2015 and in 2019 agreed to pay $37 million to settle donors' claims, without admitting wrongdoing. Short-term mission trips, taken by more than a million American church members a year by one estimate, often visit orphanages — yet UNICEF has reported that most children in orphanages have at least one living parent, the same separation of children from their fathers that Chapter Seven traced.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Paul's farewell to the elders of Ephesus was this: "I have coveted no man's silver, or gold, or apparel. Yea, ye yourselves know, that these hands have ministered unto my necessities" (Acts 20:33-34). When Paul gathered money for the poor saints, the assemblies chose the men who carried it, "Providing for honest things, not only in the sight of the Lord, but also in the sight of men" (2 Corinthians 8:21). The first man in the New Testament to carry money in the name of the poor was Judas, "because he was a thief, and had the bag" (John 12:6). Yahushua spoke of men who "devour widows' houses, and for a pretence make long prayer" (Matthew 23:14). Jude described those who came after: they "ran greedily after the error of Balaam for reward" (Jude 11). <strong>The poor have always been the easiest name to collect under.</strong> A gift sent far away lets a believer feel he has cared for the fatherless and the widow, while the widow at his own gate goes unvisited (James 1:27). <strong>A seducing spirit does not need to stop the preaching. It only needs to put the ministry up for sale, and then let anyone buy the title.</strong></p>

      <details class="cod-deepdive">
        <summary>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-label">
            <span class="cod-deepdive-icon">✦</span>
            <span class="cod-deepdive-title">Go Deeper — Heritage USA and the Lifetime Partners</span>
          </span>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-chevron">▾</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="cod-deepdive-body">
          <p class="cod-body">Jim and Tammy Faye Bakker began on Pat Robertson's Christian Broadcasting Network in the 1960s. In 1974 they started their own program in Charlotte, North Carolina, called <em>The PTL Club</em> — "Praise the Lord" or "People That Love" — and built it into a satellite network carried across the country.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">In 1978 Bakker opened Heritage USA on land in Fort Mill, South Carolina: a Christian theme park and resort with a water park, hotels, shops, a television studio, and a replica of a Jerusalem street. By the mid-1980s it was reported as one of the most visited theme parks in the United States, drawing millions of visitors a year.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">To pay for it, Bakker sold "lifetime partnerships" on the air. For a gift of $1,000, a partner was promised three nights a year in the resort's Grand Hotel for the rest of his life. Bakker told his viewers he would sell a limited number. He sold far more. Prosecutors later showed that more than 150,000 partnerships had been sold for lodging that could never hold them, while millions of dollars went to the Bakkers' salaries, bonuses, and homes. A second hotel tower promised to partners was never finished.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">In March 1987 a sexual scandal and a hidden payment to keep it quiet became public. Bakker resigned, Jerry Falwell briefly took over PTL, and within months the ministry was in bankruptcy. In October 1989 a federal jury in Charlotte convicted Bakker on all 24 counts of fraud and conspiracy. The judge sentenced him to 45 years in prison and a $500,000 fine. The sentence was later reduced, and he was paroled in 1994 after about five years.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">The people who lost their money were mostly ordinary believers — many of them retired, many on fixed incomes — who had given because a man on television told them it was for Yahuah. Paul wrote of men "supposing that gain is godliness," and told Timothy, "from such withdraw thyself" (1 Timothy 6:5). Peter warned that "through covetousness shall they with feigned words make merchandise of you" (2 Peter 2:3).</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">VI</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">Everyone Has a Ministry (1960s-Today)</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">In 1965 Donald McGavran opened the School of World Mission at Fuller Theological Seminary and taught church growth: the study of what makes congregations get bigger. In 1979 his colleague C. Peter Wagner published <em>Your Spiritual Gifts Can Help Your Church Grow</em>, and spiritual-gifts surveys spread through the churches: fill out a questionnaire, find a gift, and find the program that needs it. The movement that started by counting people ended by crowning apostles.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Scripture records what happened when a king counted the people to measure his strength: "Satan stood up against Israel, and provoked David to number Israel" (1 Chronicles 21:1), and afterward "David's heart smote him" (2 Samuel 24:10). <strong>A movement that measures the assembly by its numbers is following a seducing spirit.</strong> Yahuah sent home all but three hundred of Gideon's army so Israel could not boast in its size (Judges 7:2-7). The numbers measured how many came, never how many obeyed.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">In 1995 Rick Warren published <em>The Purpose Driven Church</em>. New members at Saddleback moved through a series of classes — Class 301 was "Discovering My Ministry" — took a SHAPE profile, and were placed on a ministry team. Churches across the country copied it. Church staff took the title too: the choir director became the "minister of music" and later the "worship pastor." Every volunteer job became a ministry, from the coffee bar to the parking lot. A believer can run the sound board faithfully for twenty years, and almost no one asks him about the ministry Scripture gave him — the temple he is, and whether he is keeping it.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">"Martha was cumbered about much serving [<em>diakonia</em>]" while Mary sat at Yahushua's feet and heard His word. He said, "one thing is needful: and Mary hath chosen that good part" (Luke 10:40-42). The one thing needful was not a job. It was a heart kept at His feet.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Yahuah rebuked His people for this: "Ye have not kept the charge of mine holy things: but ye have set keepers of my charge in my sanctuary for yourselves" (Ezekiel 44:8). <strong>This is how a seducing spirit works in a church: hand the believer a charge of the church's making, keep him busy at it for years, and leave the temple Yahuah gave him unguarded.</strong></p>

      <details class="cod-deepdive">
        <summary>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-label">
            <span class="cod-deepdive-icon">✦</span>
            <span class="cod-deepdive-title">Go Deeper — From Church Growth to Apostles: C. Peter Wagner</span>
          </span>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-chevron">▾</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="cod-deepdive-body">
          <p class="cod-body">Donald McGavran, born in India in 1897 to missionary parents, spent decades as a missionary there asking why some churches grew and others did not. His best-known answer was the homogeneous unit principle: people "like to become Christians without crossing racial, linguistic, or class barriers," so a church grows fastest when it is made of one kind of people. In 1965 Fuller Theological Seminary brought him in to found its School of World Mission. C. Peter Wagner, a missionary in Bolivia until 1971, joined him there and became the leading teacher of church growth in America.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>Signs and wonders.</strong> In 1982 Wagner and John Wimber, founder of the Vineyard churches, taught a Fuller course called "Signs, Wonders, and Church Growth," where students practiced healing in class. The seminary suspended it in 1986. In the 1990s Wagner taught "spiritual mapping": studying a city to find and name the "territorial spirits" said to rule it, so believers could pray against them by name.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>Where the five offices came from.</strong> The idea of restored apostles and prophets did not begin with Wagner. In 1948 a revival at a Bible school in North Battleford, Saskatchewan, became the Latter Rain movement. Its teachers said Yahuah was restoring the offices of Ephesians 4:11, apostles and prophets first. The Assemblies of God rejected the teaching in 1949, but the idea of a "fivefold ministry" spread through the healing revivals and the charismatic movement. Wagner gathered it up and gave it a name and an organization.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>What an apostle was.</strong> The Greek <em>apostolos</em> comes from <em>apostellō</em>, "to send": an apostle is one sent, a messenger. The Twelve were "the twelve apostles of the Lamb," witnesses of the resurrection, whose names are on the foundations of the New Jerusalem (Acts 1:21-22; Revelation 21:14). The same word is used of men sent out by an assembly: Barnabas (Acts 14:14), "the messengers of the churches" (2 Corinthians 8:23), and Epaphroditus, "your messenger" (Philippians 2:25). Neither kind ruled the assemblies: "Not for that we have dominion over your faith, but are helpers of your joy" (2 Corinthians 1:24).</p>
          <p class="cod-body">Part of the confusion is in the English. The translators wrote "apostle," a title, for the Twelve and Paul, and "messenger" for anyone else, so the same Greek word reads as an office in one verse and an errand in the next. Every believer is sent: "As my Father hath sent me, even so send I you" (John 20:21). The Twelve were the foundation, laid once and finished, with their names already on the wall. The sent ones are every believer who carries the good news, and none of them stands above another.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>Apostles.</strong> Wagner announced a "New Apostolic Reformation," with modern apostles and prophets holding authority over churches, and in 1999 founded the International Coalition of Apostles. In June 2008, at the Lakeland revival in Florida, he led a ceremony recognizing the evangelist Todd Bentley under a circle of apostles. By August, Bentley had left the revival and announced that he and his wife were separating. Yahushua commended the assembly that "tried them which say they are apostles, and are not, and hast found them liars" (Revelation 2:2).</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">VII</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Content Ministry (Today)</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">Today a ministry needs only a phone and an account. Teachers build followings on YouTube, podcasts, and social media, with a link to "support my ministry" and monthly partner pages. Many are sincere. The shape is the same one this chapter has traced: a man's name, a following, and a stream of money from the people he teaches.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Paul worked "night and day, that we would not be chargeable to any of you" and commanded, "if any would not work, neither should he eat" (2 Thessalonians 3:8, 10), and set himself against those who did otherwise: "we are not as many, which corrupt the word of God" (2 Corinthians 2:17). The Greek word is <em>kapēleuō</em> — to peddle, like a street hawker. <strong>The Word was never meant to be a product, and the teacher was never meant to be the brand.</strong> The followers count views; Yahuah counts obedience.</p>

      <p class="cod-pullquote"><strong>Servants became clergy. Clergy became a calling no one could test. The calling became a name, the name became a corporation, and every church job became a ministry. Not one step came from the apostles.</strong></p>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">VIII</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Ministry Verses</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">Several verses use <em>minister</em> and <em>ministry</em> in a way that, read quickly, sounds like a job or a career. In context they read differently.</p>

      <ul class="cod-list">
        <li><strong>"Make full proof of thy ministry" (2 Timothy 4:5).</strong> The ministry was to "preach the word" (4:2), and its order was "Take heed unto thyself, and unto the doctrine" (1 Timothy 4:16). Himself first.</li>
        <li><strong>"The ministry of the word" (Acts 6:4).</strong> It and the serving of tables are called by the same word, <em>diakonia</em> (6:1-2). There is no ladder. Stephen and Philip served tables and preached (Acts 7; 8:5).</li>
        <li><strong>"Take heed therefore unto yourselves, and to all the flock" (Acts 20:28).</strong> Yourselves first, then the flock — and in the same speech, work with your own hands (20:34-35).</li>
        <li><strong>"The ministry, which I have received of the Master Yahushua" (Acts 20:24).</strong> Paul's ministry was "to testify the gospel of the grace of God" — a witness, not an institution. He carried it while making tents.</li>
        <li><strong>"Take heed to the ministry which thou hast received in the Master" (Colossians 4:17).</strong> Archippus received his service "in the Master," not from a church program.</li>
        <li><strong>"Able ministers of the new testament; not of the letter, but of the spirit" (2 Corinthians 3:6).</strong> The letter is written "in fleshy tables of the heart" (3:3) — Jeremiah's new covenant, "I will put my law in their inward parts" (Jeremiah 31:33). The minister serves the Law moved into the heart.</li>
        <li><strong>"For the work of the ministry" (Ephesians 4:12).</strong> The teachers are given "for the perfecting of the saints, for the work of the ministry." The saints do the ministry.</li>
        <li><strong>"Let them use the office of a deacon" (1 Timothy 3:10).</strong> The word <em>office</em> is not in the Greek. It is the verb <em>diakoneō</em>, "let them serve."</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part cod-part--plain"><h2 class="cod-part-title">What Scripture Shows Instead</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">This is the ministry the Spirit of truth produces.</p>

      <ul class="cod-list">
        <li><strong>The ministry is the temple first.</strong> "Present your bodies a living sacrifice" (Romans 12:1), and "keep himself unspotted from the world" (James 1:27).</li>
        <li><strong>Keep the lamp, the bread, and the incense.</strong> "Let your light so shine" (Matthew 5:16); live on "every word that proceedeth out of the mouth of God" (Matthew 4:4).</li>
        <li><strong>From a kept temple, serve others</strong> — at home, at work, to the widow and the stranger (1 Peter 4:10).</li>
        <li><strong>The leaders work with their hands, and give</strong> (Acts 20:35).</li>
        <li><strong>No name goes on the door but His.</strong> "Do all in the name of the Master Yahushua" (Colossians 3:17).</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part cod-part--plain"><h2 class="cod-part-title">What Is the Harm?</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">Paul said the fight is "against principalities, against powers" (Ephesians 6:12). A principality does not need to stop believers from working. It only needs to point the work somewhere else. The believer is taught that ministry is a job at the church or a career for a few, and that he is faithful if he shows up for his shift — while the temple Yahuah gave him to keep, his body, his household, and his walk in the commandments, goes unguarded. The first priest was told to keep the garden, and the serpent got in anyway (Genesis 3:1). A seducing spirit has not changed its method.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Scripture shows the pattern in one man. Saul disobeyed Yahuah, kept the best of the spoil for a great sacrifice, "set him up a place" at Carmel — a monument to himself — and told the prophet, "I have performed the commandment of Yahuah" (1 Samuel 15:12-13). He believed his service was obedience. Samuel answered: "to obey is better than sacrifice … For rebellion is as the sin of witchcraft" (15:22-23). <strong>Service in place of obedience, with a monument to the man who offered it — Scripture puts that beside witchcraft.</strong></p>

      <p class="cod-pullquote"><strong>The enemy does not need to stop the believer from serving. He only needs the serving to take the place of obeying.</strong></p>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part cod-part--plain"><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Verdict</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">The ministry system is not a doctrine anyone defends. It is a practice, and so no one checks it. The record runs in one direction: a priest's service turned into a ruling class, a call no one could test, societies that answered to donors, names on the door, partner letters, titles sold by mail, and every church job renamed a ministry. Every step moved ministry from the temple to the building, from the walk to the work. That is the direction a seducing spirit moves.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Yahushua said where it ends: "Many will say to me in that day, Master, Master, have we not prophesied in thy name? … and in thy name done many wonderful works? And then will I profess unto them, I never knew you: depart from me, ye that work iniquity" (Matthew 7:22-23). The word for iniquity is <em>anomia</em>: lawlessness. <strong>They had the ministry. They did not have the walk.</strong> In the altar call the enemy erased the blood. In the ministry system he erased the walk.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Revelation shows where the true ministry ends. Those washed in the blood of the Lamb "are … before the throne of God, and serve him day and night in his temple" (Revelation 7:14-15). The word is <em>latreuō</em> — the priest's service, the same word Paul used for presenting the body. When John fell down before the angel who showed him the visions, the angel stopped him: "See thou do it not: for I am thy fellowservant … worship God" (Revelation 22:9). Even an angel would not be served in Yahuah's place.</p>

      <p class="cod-pullquote"><strong>The believer is the priest. The believer is the temple. That is the ministry. Keep it.</strong></p>

      <div class="cod-end"><span class="cod-end-mark">◆</span></div>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

